CONTEXT (from fol. 175r). Riemann is counting cyclic arrangements (necklaces) of a+b beads, a of one kind and b of the other. If gcd(a,b)=1 the number is C(a+b,a)/(a+b). For the general case he writes a = k*alpha, b = k*beta and puts v = alpha+beta. He sets z = x^(-alpha)(1+x)^(alpha+beta) with x = r e^(i phi). Writing [F] for the constant term (1/2pi) * integral of F dphi, he gets [z^k] = C(k(alpha+beta), k*alpha), which counts all linear words of length kv with content (k*alpha, k*beta). On 175r he counts words of exact period (alpha+beta), q(alpha+beta), p(alpha+beta) and pq(alpha+beta) by inclusion-exclusion, with p, q prime.

(1) LEFT COLUMN. Number of words of length kv with content (k*alpha, k*beta) whose minimal period is exactly dv. Writing Z_e = [z^e]:
- for v: [z];
- for qv: [z^q - z];
- for q^2 v: [z^(q^2) - z^q];
- for q^3 v: [z^(q^3) - z^(q^2)];
- for pqv: [z^(pq) - z^p - z^q + z];
- for pq^2 v: [z^(pq^2) - z^(pq) - z^(q^2) + z^q].
In modern terms, primitive count P(dv) = sum over e|d of mu(d/e) Z_e. This is Moebius inversion of Z_d = sum over e|d of P(ev).

(2) SCRATCH COLUMN (right). It lists, with signs reversed, the primitive counts belonging to the proper divisors of pq^2: -(z^(pq) - z^p - z^q + z), -(z^p - z), -(z^q - z), -z, -(z^(q^2) - z^q). The check is that z^(pq^2) minus all of these leaves exactly the pq^2 entry. Equivalently, the six primitive counts sum to z^(pq^2).

(3) MAIN FORMULA. Each primitive word of length dv lies in an orbit of exactly dv rotations. So for a = pq^2 alpha, b = pq^2 beta the number of necklaces is
(1/v)[ (z^(pq^2) - z^(pq) - z^(q^2) + z^q)/(pq^2) + (z^(pq) - z^p - z^q + z)/(pq) + (z^(q^2) - z^q)/q^2 + (z^p - z)/p + (z^q - z)/q + z ].
This is sum over d|N of P(dv)/(dv), with N = pq^2. Collecting coefficients (my step) gives the modern form (1/(Nv)) * sum over e|N of phi(N/e) Z_e = (1/n) * sum over d|gcd(a,b) of phi(d) C(n/d, a/d), with n = a+b.

(4) LOWER HALF: divisor sums of phi-type functions. For N = p^alpha q^beta r^gamma, Riemann writes a term of the form (p'-1)p'^(alpha-1)(q'-1)q'^(beta-1)(r'-1)r'^(gamma-1)/(p^alpha q^beta r^gamma). This is g(d)/d for a multiplicative g with g(r^j) = (r'-1) r'^(j-1); when r' = r this is Euler's phi(r^j). The superscript in the manuscript reads alpha+1, but alpha-1 is required.

The sum of g(d)/d over the divisors factors over primes. He works out the r-factor as a geometric series:
- 1 + ((r'-1)/r) * [ (r'/r)^(alpha-1) + ... + 1 ] = (r'/r - 1/r) * ((r'/r)^alpha - 1)/((r'/r) - 1) + 1;
- which equals ( (r'-1)(r'^alpha - r^alpha)/(r'-r) + r^alpha ) * r^(-alpha);
- which equals r^(-alpha) * [ (r'^(alpha+1) - r^(alpha+1))/(r'-r) - (r'^alpha - r^alpha)/(r'-r) ].
The last line expands the numerator: r'^(alpha+1) - r^alpha r' - r'^alpha + r^alpha - r^(alpha+1), with a '+' written underneath for the +r^alpha r' term that cancels. The net numerator is r'^(alpha+1) - r'^alpha + r^alpha - r^(alpha+1).

With r' = r (limit) the factor becomes 1 + alpha(1 - 1/r), which is the sum of phi(r^j)/r^j for j = 0..alpha. This is presumably aimed at simplifying the phi(N/e)/(N/e) coefficients that arise when the bracket in (3) is collected. That purpose is my interpretation; the primed variables may be meant symbolically.

(5) ISOLATED INK LINE. alpha beta * gamma delta = alpha delta * gamma beta + alpha gamma * beta delta, with xy denoting the directed segment y - x. This is Euler's four-point identity for collinear points (Ptolemy-type / three-term Pluecker relation). It is unconnected to the pencil work.
